    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In the first chapter’s discourse uses Cohen’s seven theses and Kristeva’s theories of\r\nhorror and the abject to identify a monster's features that are part of late-stage capitalism\r\nAmerican culture. In order to prevent the categorization in naming the unnamable monster, I will\r\nargue that the Moshfeghian monster’s has features of culture and will use Cohen to extrapolate on\r\nthese vortex of cultural features. However, these features will be grounded in Bellville’s practice\r\nof not naming the unnamable monster or making this being out to be purely culture. The features\r\nnoted that the Moshfeghian monster reflects onto American culture are overconsumption,\r\nwastefulness, greed, commercialization, antisemitism, racism, abjection, nuclear family,\r\nprescriptive heterosexuality, and hetero-pessimism.\r\n\r\nThe final chapter zooms out to the broader cultural and literary reception of My Year of\r\nRest and Relaxation in all kinds of readers. Positive and negative literary reviews from readers of\r\nMoshfegh’s novel on various news outlets and media platforms reveal the blind spots of\r\nsubjectivity, as is found in the reader’s relationship to the Moshfeghian monster. The reader’s\r\nperception of the Moshfeghian monster echos back their subjectivity. This subjectivity causes the\r\nreader to have a plurality of receptions that speak back to themselves, the reader’s voice, in a\r\ncyclical way. Examining a wide range of readers reviews' and their attempts to reckon with this\r\nunnamable monster reveals the infinite receptions, implications, and effects of this Moshfeghian\r\nmonster. The Moshfeghian monster ultimately avoids capture in its inability to be named by the\r\nreader, validating Moshfegh's aesthetic decision to leave unnamed her narrator protagonist. The\r\nMoshfeghian monster mirrors the culture that wishes to categorize it but cannot. Moshfegh’s\r\nnarrator is and will always remain nameless to the reader and culture. It will always escape and\r\nnever be caught, only reemerge. Ultimately, in its unnameable monstrous being, the Moshfeghian\r\nmonster is a pure monster.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
